The NW 3rd Avenue Drainage Improvements Project consists of the replacement and enhancement of the City's existing stormwater drainage system along NW 3rd Avenue between NW 23rd Street and NW 20th Street, including portions of NW 21st Court and the existing outfall to the South Fork of the Middle River. The project is designed to improve the collection, conveyance, treatment, and storage of stormwater runoff to reduce roadway flooding and enhance the resiliency of the surrounding neighborhood.
The City issued Invitation To Bid 2026-04 (ITB) for the NW 3rd Avenue Drainage Improvements Project on April 13, 2026. A total of nine (9) proposals were received in response to the ITB. The bid response from Kailas Corp met the solicitation requirements and was deemed the lowest, responsive, and responsible bidder. Following opening of bids, a discrepancy in quantities was identified and all proposers were asked to resubmit their proposed price. Upon evaluation, Kailas Corp was again ranked as the lowest, responsive, and responsible bidder with a price of $844,912.00.
Resolution No. 2026-056 and an agreement with Kailas was executed August 25, 2026, however, the original bid price of $799,042.00 was incorrectly included. This Agreement includes the correct price of $844,912.00.
Major components of the project include:
• Removal and replacement of the existing stormwater drainage system;
• Installation of new 24-inch, 30-inch, and 36-inch storm drainage piping;
• Construction of a 30-inch perforated pipe exfiltration trench to provide stormwater storage and groundwater recharge;
• Installation of new yard drains, catch basins, manholes, drainage structures, and a 24-inch inline check valve to improve hydraulic performance and reduce tidal backflow impacts;
• Reconstruction of affected water and sewer utility crossings and service connections;
• Installation of stormwater quality treatment features, including pollution-retardant baffles;
• Restoration of roadways, driveways, swales, landscaping, pavement markings, and other disturbed areas upon completion of construction.
The completed improvements will provide enhanced stormwater conveyance and attenuation for approximately 1.83 acres of roadway, reducing the frequency and severity of localized flooding while improving the long-term reliability and resilience of the City's stormwater infrastructure.

